## Docker Notes

- `compose.yml` is the production-leaning baseline
- `compose.dev.yml` is the local dev override used by the generated composer launcher flow
- `./start.sh -d` is expected to target the dev override pattern, matching the `min_survey` setup
- the reverse proxy config lives in `./.proxy/`, which holds two interchangeable options plus the shared `maintenance.html`: **Caddy** (`Caddyfile`, active) and **nginx** (`default.conf.template`, kept fully wired but commented out in `compose.yml` as a drop-in fallback). To switch, comment the `caddy:` service and uncomment `nginx:`
- Caddy runs the official `caddy:latest` image mounting `./.proxy/Caddyfile`; by default it serves plain HTTP on `:80` with `auto_https off` (so it sits behind your own TLS terminator, or is used in local dev). The published host port reads `CADDY_PORT`, then legacy `NGINX_PORT`, then `80`. To have Caddy terminate TLS itself, set `CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS` to your hostname(s), drop `auto_https off`, publish `:443`, and give the host outbound internet for ACME
- the nginx fallback mounts `./.proxy/default.conf.template` into `/etc/nginx/templates/` and renders `${NGINX_SERVER_NAME}` / `${NGINX_MAX_SIZE}` via `envsubst` at startup; tune those in `.secrets/.env`
- both proxies serve composer update progress from `dlux_runtime` at `/_update/status.json` and `/_update/log.txt`, are **not** gated on `web`'s health (`condition: service_started`), and re-resolve the recreated `web` container (Caddy natively per dial; nginx via a `resolver` directive), so the maintenance/progress page keeps polling while `web` is recreated during an image update
- point a front proxy's backend **health check** at `GET /_edge-alive` (always `200 ok`) or `GET /health` (proxies to Django normally, but returns proxy-local `200 ok` while the dlux maintenance marker exists) — not `/`, which returns 503 during maintenance so the progress page can render
- the web service image defaults to a local tag via `WEB_IMAGE`, so it is not tied to a specific Docker Hub account
- Celery uses the same app image as `web` and starts with `python -m celery -A {{ config_package }} worker -B --loglevel=info`
- Celery beat checks the DB-backed `backup_config` every 15 minutes; scheduled backups remain disabled until a superuser enables them in System Settings, and `0` retention limits mean unlimited
- `web`, `celery`, `dlux-updater`, and `composer-updater` use the same `WEB_IMAGE`; `dlux-updater` has outbound PyPI access, and `composer-updater` reaches the Docker daemon **only** through the read-only `docker-socket-proxy` (least-privilege API gateway) — the raw host socket is never mounted into an app container
- `composer-updater` is pinned to `debeski/composer:1.1.10`, excludes itself and the Docker API gateway through `COMPOSER_EXCLUDE_SERVICES=composer-updater,docker-socket-proxy`, and enables the Compose-required containers/images/networks/volumes/events/exec socket-proxy API surface
- inline (PyPI) and image (composer) updates are checked periodically but never applied automatically; only a superuser can apply/rollback from System Info after current-password confirmation
- the runtime volume retains every verified release and survives container recreation; the baked image package remains the fallback
- keep the compose file in the standard inline-env pattern; do not wire `.secrets/.env` into services directly

## Generated Env File

The generated `.secrets/.env` includes only these bootstrap values:

- `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY`
- `POSTGRES_USER`
- `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`
- `PGADMIN_DEFAULT_EMAIL`
- `PGADMIN_DEFAULT_PASSWORD`
- `ADMIN_PASS`
- `DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL`
- `SMTP_RELAY_HOST`
- `SMTP_RELAY_PORT`
- `SMTP_RELAY_USE_TLS`
- `SMTP_RELAY_USER`
- `SMTP_RELAY_PASSWORD`

The SMTP relay values are bootstrap/fallback values. For the normal UI-first path,
open System Setup step 3, choose delivery path `Internal SMTP relay`, choose
secret storage `Encrypted database secret`, and enter the upstream SMTP provider
settings there.

Keep `.secrets/.env` out of version control and replace all default passwords before deployment.

## App Workflow

- Create new apps with `python -m dlux startapp app_name`
- Use `python -m dlux startapp app_name --register` if you want DjangoLux to patch `INSTALLED_APPS` and project URLs for you
- Generated apps follow the discovery-friendly Dlux layout: model-adjacent forms, filters, tables, views, translations, templates, and tests
- Keep app-local translations in `translations.py`
- Keep forms, filters, and tables in their conventional files so DjangoLux discovery can find them automatically

More project-specific guidance lives in [docs/README.md](docs/README.md).
